      "assistant_text": "## The Connection\n\nThese two decisions are complementary architectural patterns that both depend on a **single composition point**:\n\n**Credential Scoping** ([[concepts/credential-scoping]]) isolates secrets at the process boundary \u2014 the credential never lives in the Hub\u0027s environment and gets injected into child processes (Ingest and Query agents) only at spawn time.\n\n**Runtime Path Configuration** ([[concepts/runtime-paths]]) centralizes *all* file-system location decisions into one startup resolver, preventing path logic from scattering across the codebase.\n\nBoth patterns share the same underlying design principle: **consolidate decisions at a single entry point, then distribute the results downstream without allowing components to re-derive them.** \n\nFor credential scoping, this means the Hub\u0027s startup sequence decides how and where to load secrets, then passes only the relevant credential into each child process\u0027s environment \u2014 child processes never ask \u0022where should I find the API key?\u0022\n\nFor runtime paths, the resolver at startup answers \u0022where is the wiki root?\u0022, \u0022where is the database?\u0022, and \u0022where do my instructions live?\u0022 \u2014 once, centrally \u2014 so no component downstream has to guess or rediscover those locations.\n\nThe two decisions work in tandem: when you spin up the Query agent (a runtime path), you *also* inject its credential into that specific child process (credential scoping). Neither concern leaks out to decide itself \u2014 both flow from the Hub\u0027s single composition point.\n\n---\n\n**This is genuinely worth recording.** I\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]], because it articulates how these two architectural decisions are not independent but rather manifestations of the same underlying principle \u2014 a pattern that should inform similar decisions elsewhere in the system.\n\nLet me now record this synthesis:",
