        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01AXb4X6G8tpocyFGUu4PEPj",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point\\ndescription: How credential scoping and runtime-path decisions both consolidate at a single startup entry point to prevent downstream rediscovery.\\ntimestamp: 2025-04-10T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - pattern/Centralization\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both source pages explicitly describe consolidation at a single entry point; the pattern is their stated design principle.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2025-10-10\\n---\\n\\n## The Pattern\\n\\nBoth [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] follow the same architectural principle: **consolidate all decisions of a particular kind at a single startup entry point, then distribute the results downstream without allowing components to re-derive them.**\\n\\n## Credential Scoping\\n\\nThe Hub\u0027s startup sequence determines how to load the Anthropic API credential (from a git-ignored \u0060.env\u0060 file), then injects it only into the environment of spawned child processes (Ingest and Query agents) at process creation time. The credential never exists in the Hub\u0027s own process environment and child processes never ask \\\u0022where should I find the API key?\\\u0022 \u2014 they receive it as a given.\\n\\n## Runtime Paths\\n\\nA single path resolver at Hub startup reads configuration (command line, environment, config file, code defaults), validates every required location exists, auto-creates writable directories as needed, and reports the effective value and source of each path. No downstream component reads the ambient working directory directly; everything consumes the already-resolved path set instead.\\n\\n## Why This Matters\\n\\nThis pattern prevents two failure modes:\\n\\n1. **Scattering logic**: If path resolution or credential discovery lived in multiple components, each would have to implement the same logic, creating inconsistency and maintenance burden.\\n2. **Rediscovery overhead**: Allowing components to re-derive decisions at runtime invites inconsistency (especially across process boundaries) and obscures the true composition dependency graph.\\n\\nBy contrast, a single composition point makes the Hub\u0027s dependency structure explicit: credentials and paths are properties of the whole system, determined once, then distributed as immutable inputs to child processes and components.\\n\\n## Application\\n\\nWhen adding a new runtime location (e.g., the Query agent\u0027s instruction directory or artifact storage), you extend the path resolver, not scatter new path logic into the agent code. Similarly, when adding a new credential-dependent component, you arrange injection into its process environment at spawn, not discovery within the component itself.\\n\u0022}"
        },
